  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Research on Gendered Reintegration Experiences and Gender-Sensitive, Gender-Responsive and Gender-Transformative Approaches to Reintegration</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2021, IOM in collaboration with the Maastricht Graduate School of Governance of Maastricht University conducted a study that analysed reintegration outcomes through a gender perspective. The study provided new insights into gendered reintegration experiences and how gender relates to reintegration sustainability. However, there are still evidence gaps relating more practically to how reintegration programmes can effectively support women and girls as well as returnees with diverse gender identities and expressions, and sex characteristics (SOGIESC).The German Federal Ministry for Economic Cooperation and Development (BMZ) and IOM will jointly work on a new research study that examines gendered reintegration experiences and identifies gender-sensitive, gender-responsive and gender-transformative approaches that empower women and returnees with diverse SOGIESC in their reintegration. This study will look specifically at good practices and developing concrete recommendations for policy and programming. Apart from further expanding the knowledge base on gender and reintegration, in practice, the results can be used by practitioners to adapt reintegration measures to the needs of different target groups and therefore offer more inclusive and tailor-made reintegration programmes.</narrative>
  </description>
